# Cloudflare Pages redirects.
#
# /p is where a scanned pairing code lands. It is a real path rather than a
# hash because a phone's camera opens the QR's URL directly, and the fragment
# after it — which carries the box's key — never reaches this server.
#
# 200 rather than 301: the fragment must survive, and the app reads it on the
# client. A redirect would work too, but rewriting keeps the URL the user
# scanned in the address bar, which is what they can check against the box.
/p /index.html 200

# /a is where an assistant's consent page at FTW Cloud sends the owner, with
# the request and the signed offer in the fragment, which never reaches this
# server. The iPhone app opens it when installed (apple-app-site-association).
# Anywhere else this small page says to open it on the iPhone with FTW; it
# never reads the fragment, and the web app has no part in approving. There
# is no /a/<request>: a request in a path would land in a log.
/a /access-link.html 200

# Everything else is hash-routed inside the shell, so there is no SPA fallback
# to add. A request for an unknown path is genuinely not found.
